% Mortality Act
%
% The Logical English twin of a Blawx encoding, written by the Blawx translator
% (InsurLE2/migration/blawx). Each rule is a rule of the Act as Blawx encodes it,
% in the words of Blawx's own sentence forms, citing its section; the project's
% tests are the scenarios, with Blawx's own answers as the expectations.
%
% Source: the Blawx project "Mortality Act" (mortality.yaml, 2 sections), an example of
%         Blawx v1.6.22-alpha (Lexpedite, MIT licence), commit 3de892f67854292b304c9a55c5e2cd2058d3d418.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a thing* is a mortal.
    *a thing* is a human; scenario element.

the knowledge base mortality includes:

the text of the Mortality Act is at "sources/mortality.md".

the text of the Blawx project is at "sources/mortality.yaml".

% The Act, section by section.
rule section_1 with provenance the Mortality Act at section 1:
a thing is a mortal if
    the thing is a human.

scenario socrates is, as stated in the Blawx project at test Socrates:
    socrates is a human.
    socrates expects answers ["socrates is a mortal"].

scenario case_1 is, as stated in the case analysis of the encoding at section 1:
    % a case of the rule of section 1 (blawx_cases.pl), answered by Blawx
    thing_a is a human.
    which_is_a_mortal expects answers ["thing_a is a mortal"].

query socrates is:
    which thing is a mortal.

query which_is_a_mortal is:
    which thing is a mortal.

the view the act is:
    the title is "Mortality Act, as Blawx encodes it".
    the case is a scenario, with the documents it is stated in.
    the facts about "the case" are
        a thing is a human.
    the result is the answer to query which_is_a_mortal.
    the result shows its reasons.
    the result shows its citations.
    the result can be flipped.

